"What's a saint?"
Now Trump’s White House has setttling the Statue of Svoboda.
The most trustworthy sign of regime change is when it begins to deal with the statues.
The Americans understand this no worse than everyone else - hence the intentional theatrical demolition of Saddam Hussein's Statuty on Firdos Square in Baghdad in 2003.
Steven Miller, one key ideologure of the Trump government, no doubt knew what he was doing when he brought a symbolic appoint of the Statue of Liberty during a bitter argument with CNN journalist Jim Acosta about the president’s proposals to drastically reduce legal migration.
Let's be honest, Miller didn't encroach on the statue.
Regillations of the owners of pleasure boats and artists depicting the Statue of Svoboda would shaltize him if he had tried to do it.
But he encroached on its meaning, and especially the meaning that was attributed to her in 1903, 17 years after completing the Monument, when germs of Emma Lazarus were added to the pedestal.
“I don’t want to go deep down here, but the Statue of Liberty is ... a symbol of American freedom that illuminates the world.
The empiration you are talking about was added later and is not part of the original Statue of Sweezers.”
Millère correctly stated the facts, but as he himself said, it’s not really about the story.
It's about the current sound of the astounding words of Lazarus, the one in which the state invites not any immigrant, but the poorest: "Your tiding, your poor, / your maids who want to sneak up freely, / Unhappy scatnasions of your plentiful shores."
I've never seen "unfortunate scatters" like that on our screen.
Millère was not engaged in literary criticism - he made it clear that in America these people are not waiting.
It’s a part of a broader political and cultural stage dispute: it’s a debate about the meaning of America.
Lázázáz - and her friends, who struggled to put her words on the basement of the statue after her death - understood very well that they were committing an absolutely political act.
Sculpture according to the plan was supposed to indicate the connection between the French and American republics with the help of a banal classical figure - the female image of Liberty.
Lazarus has changed its meaning: in her poem the female figure is no longer abstract, she has a voice.
And she received a completely different name: The Mother of the Exiles.
Compilation of the epoch and image was a gorgeous feminist move and a devastating attack against American nativism.
And how Lazarus changed the meaning of the statue, although the right ones change it back.
Cycling Rush Limbo, such as John the Baptist, proclamed the arrival of Trump, in 2010 reasoned that freedom does not invite anyone, but rather it is an early neo-conservative, carrying the flame of America lying in the darkness of the world: “Freedom is moving forward.
She bears the torch of freedom from the United States to the rest of the world.
The torch doesn’t be needed to cover the way to the U.S.”
Opposite the habit of Limbo here really came to the point.
The meaning of the statue depends solely on the angle of view.
Lazarus’ great creative accomplishment was to see her as it would have been seen by emasculated, miserable but hopeful people on the decks of the ships after a long and often heated voyage.
This means to literally look at the world through the eyes of people who seek refuge and a chance for a better life.
Its statue responds to them the words of hope and hospitability, which they dreams to hear.
And, of course, this is not the prospect that Miller and trump will ever want to recognize.
Their torch is necessary not to illuminate the way, but to incite a hatred, which is the only source of their power.
