“Captain of the national team is chosen by other members of the team, so in any case it was a huge honor, but to know that your comrades voted for you and you have chosen – for me this is a very special thing,” she added.
“Of course, I’m very proud of all the Scots who entered the national team.
This shows how much we have achieved.
And they're all anxious attentive athletes who will strive to get to the pedest of honor.
What do you mean by my captain's speech?
Yeah, I think it wasn't bad.
I just paid attention to the fact that we were very lucky to host the World Cup at home.
So it’s just about how important it is to get support from the fans and take advantage that we will again find ourselves at the Olympic Stadium.
I got another chance to enter his arena and take part in the competition, and the atmosphere there will just be wonderful.”
Bloating Critical Critique on Facebook: You Don't Need to Be Published If You Aren’t Ready to Listen to Criterity from Voters
The founder of our country understood what a critical press is.
The Americans loved George Washington, but it didn’t prevent them from criticizing it from domestic politics to its political principles.
Criticism annoyed him, of course.
He didn’t stick his mouth to his critics.
Because in 1783, Washington said: “You can take away the freedom of speech of us, defiscated and dumb, will lead as a sheep to the slaughter.”
Now I’m moving on to Maryland Governor Larry Hoaghan, who should be more like Washington.
Subordinate Jogan blocked and erased the posts of at least 450 people, who expressed their opinion on his official Facebook page.
And this week the American Civil Liberties Union sued him.
Subordinaries of the governor called this claim negligible, and the comment on the Internet was written in the vein of “well and what”.
“It’s only Facebook,” a lot of people say.
But that's what's important too.
And this is especially important when it’s a Hogan-like guy.
He is an amazingly popular republican gubernatorium in a predominantly democratic state.
He is in the second place in the approval rating among the 50 governors of the country.
Hogan is not a stunted responding shareholder.
He demonstrated a firm hand in the management of the state and adherence to his principles.
He also deftly uses Facebook as an primordial way of connecting with their constituents and with the jokes on his wigglestling his bald after chemotherapy.
Therefore, to block the people who come to the page of the governor - which is stated as an official public forum and is conducted by administration employees with the money of taxpayers - it is unnecessary and in the final analysis is harmful.
In an interview with “Washington Post”, the press Secretary Amelia Chez defended the actions of the governor, arrguing that blocking the comments is simply their moderation.
But it is too easy to use an image of trolls, spammers and haters who magnify on the Internet.
"Post" spoke with some of the real people whom Hogan blocked.
And these are really real people, not trolls - a teacher, a businesswoman, a pastor who turn to their chosen leaders.
All of them say that their comments were respectful, informative and not rude.
Pastor quoted the Biblue, appealing that Hogan is a Catholic.
Lawyer Lakshimi Sarma Ramana of Bowie, Maryland, didn't express hatred, but asked about hate crimes.
“I wrote a polite comment that I am frustrated with the absence of his response to hate crimes and to other recent news,” she wrote in the comment section of the article in “Poston”.
“I don’t even like the idea that when a lot of people leaves comments on the same topic, some immediately say that it’s a so-called cheat, instead of admitting that they are dealing with a large group of dissatisfied citizens.”
The fact that the subordinates of the gubernator are called “concentrated spam-attack”, others, probably, would be called an “active life position”.
In the era of Facebook it is easy to re-edit your image by simply blocking critics or washeing negative comments.
It’s a much less dusty job than it used, when it’s required to send people, collect and burn newspapers with critical editorials, or arrest and shutting the protests.
But this is exactly what’s going on now, only in numbers.
Hogen isn’t the first official to criticise him don’t want to listen to Facebook and other social networks.
President trump was sudated against Twitter users who were banned in his Twitter account.
One of the first reversal decisions on this topic was made this week in Virginia.
According to U.S. District Judge of Alexandria James C. Cecheris, the chairman of the Supervisory Board Laudon violated the First Amendment, banishing the elector in his Facebook account.
And in Kentucky, the Republican Governor Matt Beavin filed a claim from the U.S. Civil liberties union for his behavior on Facebook and Twitter.
It doesn't have to be that hard.
During Washington's time, the era of available postal expenses carried out approximately the same upheaval as the Internet today.
From 1776 to 1800, the number of newspapers doubled four times, and the authors of the anonymous letters greatly brought his authority.
And even at the same time Washington dealt with anonymous trolls.
People under the pseudonyms “Juricola”, “Valerius”, “Belicarius” and “Porcius” wrote letters in the newspapers ridiculing the decision of Washington.
According to historical documents compiled by George's Washington Documents online project, his cabinet was littered with petitions criticizing his position on the Treaty of Amity, Commerce and Navigation.
He didn’t try to silence them.
The freedom of speech, dissent and speaking lies at the very basis of our state.
And true leadership means accepting it.
