The Poverty and Demise in the Indonesian “Golden Land”
Preparing lunch, Bardine Degea doesn't use a stove.
It even rarely uses the pot.
In her wooden house in Enarotali, Paniya County’s capital in the rebellious Indonesian province of Papua, a housewacher usually just puts sweet potato, which’s called “Note”, right in the hearth.
After half an Hurricane blaspbery, take out and ate impatient, unwashed hands.
Degaya sits on an earthen floor - she does not have furniture - and this is where she performs her work around the house, such as washeing clothes with turbulent water from a nearby swamp.
The bathroom serves a vein in a vein without a roof.
She is the junior of her husband's four wives, so she wasn't instructed to work in the pitch.
Nudcies are prevalentiated.
Of course, it can be dangerous to work late: many men from this village are unemployed, many people are drunk, and there are also soldiers.
"No one dares to walk through the village after five in the evening," she says.
It’s a raisical opportunity to look at everyday life in Papua highlands, a former Dutch colony that was annexed to Indonesia in 1969 after a dubious referendum when only 1026 of the Elders were forced to vote, publicly raising his hands in the occupying troops.
Existing movement advocating independence from the Netherlands has quickly angered Jakarta’s government, which maintains strict control of the region, preventing foreign journalists and human rights defenders.
In the year of 2003 the province was officially divided into Papua and West Papua, and an independent state of Papua New Guinea occupies an eastern part.
Enorotali - the city is as removable as an abandoned one; the road here includes one and a-and-a-half flight from Jayapura, the administrative center of the province, in Nabira, and then - a five-hour shaking on a hired car.
There's no public transport.
The city with a population of about 19 000 people consists of wooden houses enclosed with bamboo fences; roofs made of corrugated steel are painted rust in a variety of gurgulated shades.
Few people of the Indonesians came here, not to mention journalists, and almost none of the foreigners came here.
Prior to the arrival of Christian missionaries, papouas worshipped a god named Uga Tame.
There have been other changes.
“It wasn’t annoyed to wear these clothes,” says Dehei, pointing at his bright, hand-woven turban, dark skirt and a vibrant shirt.
“Perhaps we wore only the leaves on the body.”
Papua is Indonesia’s poorest-dollar province, 28% of its population lives below the poverty line, with child mortality and literacy rates among the worst in Asia.
It’s an Indonesian golden land.
The world's largest and most lucrative golden pultum, Grasberg, located in the Phoenix "Freeport McMoran", is located just sixty-mile from Paniai, a high-altitude province of about New Jersey, where 153 000 people live.
Only in 2015, Freeport produced gold and copper here about $ 3.1 billion.
In addition, Papua boasts timber reserves, the cost of which is estimated at $ 78 billion.
These richevcies, however, entail one misfortune for Papuans, guaranteeing that Indonesia's powerful armed forces maintain a suffocating presence.
According to an investigation of the New York Times in 2005, between 1998 and 2004 Freeport paid almost $ 20 million to local servicemen and subdivides, including up to $150,000 to one officer.
Demands of greater autonomy by Papuans threatening the existence of this golden vein and mercilessly being suppressed.
According to human rights activists, more than 500,000 Papuans have been murdered by Indonesian military since 1969, and thousands more have been raped, tortured and deprived of their liberty.
According to the Asian Commission on human rights, the mass homicide of Papua highly inhabited highlands in the seventies can be considered a genocide.
Just last year, Indonesian police arrested more than 3,3900 peaceful protesters in the region.
“We will Lose Everything”, a report published in 2016 by the Diocese of Brisbane Contains Evidence of Atrocities Committed Committed In The Previous YEAR, Such as Executed Execution – Particularly Frequently Charactided – Especially Frequently Pronounced by another report, rape and electrocution – and the brutal suppression of peaceful demonstrations.
“It’s hard to count the number of victims because the incidents take place every week,” says Andreas Harzon, who studies in Indonesia for the Human Rights Watch.
Nuger queevue was delayed, since Papua resources attracted an influx of immigrants from other areas of Indonesia.
The 3.5-million provincial population consists of 83% Christians, but demographics change as Muslim economic migrants come from densely populated Indonesian islands—Java, Sumatra and Sulawesi.
Yavan benches, "warungians", sell fried chicken and a mixture of vegetables "gato-yad", supplied with peanut sauce.
Local residents are trying to withstand the competition.
“Migrants started selling chicken and vegetables on the traditional market cheaper than the Native Papua people,” Explains Abet Yow, a twenty-four-year-long native of Paniai, who moved to Jayapura, the administrative center of the province because of his work.
Because of this, the indigenous residents of Papua - mother-mother (women) Papua - have lost their sales market."
Indonesian President Joko Widódo, popularly known as Djokovi, vowed to deal with inequalities and human rights in Papua during his 2014 presidential campaign.
On his way to Jakarta's Presidential Palace, the former carpenter enlisted the support of 27 of Papua 29 counties, including Pariaya.
But in Papua almost nothing has changed, and the locals feel betrayed.
“Our hearts are shattered, because in 2014 we voted for Jokovi, expecting that he will fulfill our hopes for the restoration of fairness,” says Yow.
In fact, only two months after the October inauguration of Djokovi Paniai survived the tragedy.
December 7 December 2014, a group of 11 children was on the street and sang Christmas hymns at the  cockshop in Enarotali, when two Indonesian soldiers broke out of the dark on a motorcycle.
Frightened children told them they should turn the headlimp on.
Local pastor Javedt Taubai reported, one of the soldiers was offended by their tone and after a while returned with four soldiers.
According to victims and eyewitnesses, the soldiers who were drunk were chasing the children and beating them with rifles.
Then one of the soldiers shot at a group of children.
One, a sixteen-year-old Julianus Eumo, was beaten so badly that he had fallen into someone.
A couple of hours later, someone set fire to the nearby government building of the electoral commission, and the next day the situation escalated.
Approximately a thousand papouas men, women and children gathered in the football field in front of a local police station and a military command center to demand justice.
They brought with them ritual hunting bulbs and smuggled a white, the dance of the Papuan tribe of the cerepoan pago: they ran in circles and depicted bird singing.
Some of the protesters began throwing the stones to the police station and a military base.
“If the motto resists more than three times, they will shoot them,” he said, according to an official document that was seen by TIME, but which has not been published.
Fifty-six-year-old Yeremias Kayama, the head of Kugo Koto district in Enarotali, felt threatened and called for tranquillity, appealing to the mottle with a request to disperse to their homes.
No one wanted to listen to him.
“When I turned, a bullet suddenly hit my left wizard in my left wizard,” he told TIME magazine, standing on the porch of his brightly painted wooden house.
Kayama still doesn't even know who shot, but says that the bullet has flown the rank of assembled soldiers.
There was a mob, there were a lot of shots,” he adds.
In a local inhabitant Alfius Youva were hit three times, according to his cousin, who witnessed the firing.
"I ran up to him and examined his body to make sure it was him," Johannes greeply told TIME magazine, who, like many Indonesians, uses only one name.
“I’ve Seen He’s Blew Dead... I Kiss Him.”
General Yotje Méné, Papua Chief Police Inspector, told reporters that his officers had just “defended” their base because it had been attacked.
“We have to defend ourselves when people threatening to kill us,” Papua police spokeswoman Pudjo Sulistilio said in a statement.
“It’s a Matter of Living and Detente.”
According to Human Rights Watch, five young protesters have been killed and much more have been injured.
News about the assassinations reached Jakarta only the next day.
After three weeks, Jocovi delivered an terrific speech in Jayapur, in which he expressed condolences for the families of the victims and swore to address the issue of historical abusions of power in Papua.
“I want this situation to be settled immediately, so that it will never again happen again in the future,” he said.
In October of 2016, Minister for Security Viranto said that he would establish a non-judicial organization to put an end to historical violations of human rights.
But almost immediately the excuses went out.
“More part of the violations occurred a long time ago.
Some of them took place in the twelfth tieth or at the beginning of zero.
The fact is that, of course, we really want to deal with these violations, but there are procedures that need to pass,” he said.
Viranto then renounced his own words, speaking to TIME magazine in Jakarta on June 5, and said that he did not intend to establish an organization to deal with Papua complaints.
Instead, he said, “Everything will be decided through the trial.”
Viranto, whom the United Nations accused of “criminals against humanity” for more than 1,000 killed in East Timor in 1999, said that 11 human rights violations in Papua had already been settled, including an incident in Pania.
Families of the victims from Paniya met with such statements with greed disbelief.
“Over the past three years, I’ve been questioned four times, but there hasn’t been absolutely any change,” Johannes said.
"I'm Tired."
He's saying he's still living in terrified years later.
“I’m scared,” he said.
"I'm afraid that I'm being arrested by the military, I'm afraid I'm going to be shot."
His brother Yakobus also expressed the opinion that the inhabitants of Paniyai are afraid to discuss the incident.
He said he'd be beaten the military after he helped bury the four victims.
“Once we buried the bodies, the military came for me,” he said.
Shooting didn't stop.
On Tuesday, Indonesian police shot at village dwellings in neighboring Paniya district of Deyai.
During the cloacification between villagers and the manager of the construction company, which refused to help take the man unconscious to the hospital, one person was killed and 17 others were injured, including children.
The man, twenty-four-year-large Ravianus Dow, drowned while catching fish in a nearby river, died on the way to the hospital.
Angrigated residents of the village protested in front of the office of a construction site owned by the company.
According to the police, residents of the village threw stones at the officers, who in response fired warning shots.
But local residents say that the mobile brigade (Indonesian special forces) began to shoot at the crowd and killed one person.
“We were so in a jerk, we are afraid of retaliation,” Dominggu Badi, told TIME’s twenty-nine-year-old Dominggu, who lives near a hospital and saw them hurriedly deliver the wounded.
“I huddled at my house for two days.”
Deiya Parliament demanded that the officers involved in the incident be called for an answer, and the mobile police brigade was withdrawn from the region.
Paniya has always been a painful place for the Indonesian government.
The disadvantage of significant development causes dissatisfaction of tribes, moni, tribute and dala, scattered throughout the engratled green-cross-covered Papua.
Many have acceded to the Papua Freight Movement (OPM), an Insurgent Army, which claims to protect papouay rights by singling sporadic attacks and raids with kidnapping on Indo-Jendoian soldiers.
Some top OPM leaders come from Paniya, including Tadius Yogi and Daniel Judas Kogoya.
As a retaliatory measure in recent years, Thousands of people have been arrested and unreasonably detained by the military under the pretext of “defending national independence”.
Some didn't come back.
Papua Paniya is considered a “several, forgotten place.”
Poverty exacerbates dissatisfaction.
A small portion of rice sold in Enarotali is too expensive for locals.
Bakery is equally unavailable.
People here grow everything that they eat: mainly a note, as well as some fruits and leafy vegetables.
Rural agriculture is the work of women, each of which can take care of four or five fields of sweet potatoes.
Usually they retain most of the harvest for the family, and the rest is sold in the local market.
The ten strawberries of a note are only 10 000 Indonesian rupees (75 cents).
Over time, economic inequalities increased between Native Papuans and new migrants, who arrived in large numbers after the introduction of new flights to Nabier airport.
Few existing vacancies usually go to more well-educated and affigious migrants.
Papuans smiltly have enough money or the necessary skills to conduct their own competitive business.
“Yellow people don’t want to stay in the village  ... because there’s not a job or no money here,” says John Gobai, chairman of the tribal council.
Isolation keeps Papua away from the views of the rest of the world.
In addition, the restrictions on the work of the international media remain rigid.
Earlier this year, French journalists Frank Esciudi and Bazil Lonshan were deported from Papua “insufficient coordination with the relevant organizations” despite being issued rare permits for video footage.
According to Felym Kinet, Human Rights Watch’s deputy director for Asia, pledges made during Djokovi’s election campaign to unleavengthen restrictions on media work in order to promote transparency and development have not been implemented.
“Foreign journalists attempting to report from the incoming Eastern Indonesian provinces, Papua and West Papua now, face new dangers: visa denial and blacklisting,” he said in a statement.
Dissatisfaction from the press means that international pressure on the government of Indonesia is mainly limited to Papua’s immediate neighbors.
In March, there were six Pacific states – Tonga, Nauru, Palau, Tuvalu, the Marshall Islands and the Solomon Islands – appealed to the UN Human Rights Council with a request to investigate “variety and widespread violations” in Papua Papua, including filament in Pania.
In the past, the same countries supported OPM in the past.
A spokeswoman for the Indonesian Foreign Ministry, Arramana Nassir brushed abdicated the group’s statement, informing reporters in Jakarta that “There’s still a democratic system and there’d still be a free-hearced press, so it would be difficult to hide evidence of human rights violations.”
Local residents want a larger number of foreign governments to draw attention to the situation.
When an official delegation from the Netherlands, led by the Dutch human rights ambassador, Kiiss Van Baar, visited Jayapura on May 4, the locals broke the silence, begging: “We want freedom”, according to a source who was also present at the meeting but wished to remain announcing.
In Indonesia in 2019, new presidential elections will take place, but papouans say it is unlikely to vote for Jokovi again.
“Jokovi is a person who has good intentions, but he is surrounded by people involved in the shooting in Paniyai,” says Gobai, chairman of the tribal council.
He wants Djokovi to know that the people of Paniya, in addition to living in the shadow of threats from the greedy army, immersed themselves in the poverty, and their education and healthcare are in a pitiful state.
Gobai says that the inhabitants of Paniai, like other papuAs, consider their voice given for Jokovi a debt that he has to pay.
“They don’t need money, they just want justice,” he says.
Despite jeopardics and intimidation, the families of the victims of the fiscenaries in Paniya carried out the last symbolic act of disobedience: buried the body of one of the victims on a plot of land directly in front of a police and a military precinct.
Knowing that justice will probably never prevail, they, at least, will not allow those guilty to forget about their crimes.
“Have been killed by a member of our family,” says Jakobus, not raising his head.
“What else did we have to do?”
The fire for the for the second time covers the Dubai skyscraper "Fakel"
The fire engulfed one of the world's tallest residential skyscrapers in Ouqai on Friday, forcing hundreds of residents hurriedly to leave a seventy-for-fashion-storey structure of 337 meters.
This is already the second-largest bang that swept through a high-altitude turret - which coincidentally is called "Torch" - for two years.
