Murder with Morse Ford: Activists will lose Lynch on the stage to call for justice
"Ford Mercoury" in 1948 drove past spectators in the village of Monroe in the state of Georgia and rushed with a roar to a small bridge Mors Ford.
When the car, in which there were four black passengers and a white driver, drove up to the bridge, a group of white men with guns barred her way.
The main - a middle-aged man in a suit in a thin strewed - dragged on a cigar and looked through the windshield.
Sitting inside terrified with fright.
"We need this nigger, Roger!" the man said.
"Go him out of the car!"
Piercing clumses tore the silence, when the young black man was pulled out of the forebrain.
Luckily, this horrific scene, which played out on July 22, just a staging.
But when a noose was thawed on Roger's neck, almost all viewers actually held their breath.
Since the year of 2005, the activists have returned to the Mors Ford Bridge to re-lose the night when two black couples lynched two black coupes in 1946 - Roger and Dorothy Malcolm and May Murray Dorthsy.
No one was convicted of these murders.
“It’s astonishing that over the course of all these years, no one has been arrested – even though we see them next to us; even though we know who they are,” says Tyron Brooks, who was assisting.
“It’s a stain on our history and it’s a stone on our souls.”
Brooks, 71-year-old Brooks is a former member of the Congress of Georgia, who has been wrestling for human rights all his life.
The spectacle for him is a dramatic call for action and an annual reminder to Monroe that injustice has never been corrected.
“We want an investigation, we want an end, we want to be healed, we want reconciliation, but first we have to achieve justice,” he says.
“We can’t go to healing and reconciling until we achieve justice.”
The coroner overwhelms the body of one of lynching victims at Morse Ford on July 27, 1946.
According to information by the FBI, obtained by the BBC Newes, more than 50 people in Monroe and neighboring counties were suspected of participating in lynching.
But no one has ever been charged.
Although the suspects have remained at large for decades, human rights activists say that the city always knew exactly the two associated motivarian motivators: politics and sex.
In April 1946, the ruling of the Supreme Court for the first time allowed black citizens to vote in the primaries, which were to take place in that summer.
Approroximately during the same election, according to reports by the FBI, a black proponent Roger Malcolm stabbed Barnette Chester, a white landlord - in a vomit, because of a woman.
Brooks says Hester slept with Malcolm Dorothy’s wife and that the child she was supposed to give birth was not from her husband.
On July 25, 1946, Loya Harrison, a large white landlord, reportedly paid $600 bail to rescue Malcolm out of prison.
He went to Jail with Doroth and their cousins May Murray and George Dorthrie.
But when they were traveling from the prison and drove up to the Morse Ford Bridge, a mottle of the liners surrounded the car.
“It turns it out that he organized and commanded Harrison’s assassinations,” an FBI report concluded.
“It is also obvious that there was collusion between state-of-ceremonies, which not only took part in the homicides but also destroyed the evidence and hid the identity of the witnesses.”
Brooks calls it shorter.
"It was an attempt on the suffrage," he says.
“They’ve all been killed to send a signal to the blacks in the city: if you enjoin and vote, you’ll have the same.”
Cassandra Green has spent almost a decade leading an annual performance reproducing that lynching at the Morse Ford Bridge.
Its performance is visible, it captures the spectator and narrates about the profound racism, which, as many would wish to consider, is no longer in America.
It’s hard to watch it and it’s even more difficult to turn away, but green thinks it’s important.
“We don’t want to talk about Lynch’s courts because they show us our own ugliness,” she said.
"But I can't build a relationship with you if you don't listen to me or don't hear what I was going through."
The rehearsals of the play of this year began in June, and on the first day almost 30 actors - a dozen whites, the rest of the blacks - gathered in the installement and listened polished during the years the review of the play.
Wade Marbo was a director with Green.
For many years he played the role of the main member of the clan, but says that the role has never gottententententen more easily.
“It seems to me that I was slept, because it’s not the right person that I am,” says Marbo.
“But I think it’s very important to remember this story because we don’t want to go back at that time.”
In the installement, actors Darrius Bradshaw and Nick Ruscona, who play Roger Malcolm and Barnett Hester, replicate their roles.
The spectacle begins when Malcolm, engraved that Hester sleeps with his wife Dorothy, is screaming and pushing him.
"I have said shit, get out of my house!"
Screaming Rasnova in return.
Strike is given.
And in the same moment, everyone (at least all the black actors) erupt with a laugh.
Green laughs the loudest.
"What is it," Askona asks.
She's worried and embarrassed.
Am I replaying?
"No," says green through laughter.
"I just don't think the whites then told 'Nnegg'.
"Ye-y-y-y-y-yy-y-y-y-yeth," laughs one of the black performers.
"You must piss off a solid r."
And the energy in the room also changes: the discomfort is resonated by a sense of camarade.
Everyone should do everything right, not only for the sake of the play, but also for the memory of the four killed people.
Scene starts again.
This time, when Rascona speaks the word "n", it turns out a clear and abrupt.
No one is laughing.
The spectacle ends violently and quickly.
Screams, shots, and then silence.
The woman in mourning black clothes stands above the bodies of the actors and sings the sad song of Billy Holiday "Strange Fruit".
Her voice is interrupted through the word.
Hundred quirds, fifty spectators wipe tears and sweat from it.
Among the spectators almost an equal number of black and white.
A couple came from Ithaca, New York, to see a performance; many live very close.
They endured almost eight hours the hard cassulus of Georgia, while Brooks took them to the pilgrimage to Malcolm and Dorthy.
Until this point, the murders remained only a story, but now that they are standing on the same place where people ly seed, it seems that their ghosts float immediately in the air.
When the last note of the song subsides, Green invites people to come closer and see the bodies.
The children bend to their heads, touch the paint depicting the blood, and immovable bodies whisper something to each other.
"They're not really dead," one little boy mutters, as if to remind himself.
After a few minutes, Grin thanks the spectes the spectators, and suddenly everything ends.
Actors throw ropes from the neck and wiping away tears.
Bradshaw hugs Marbo, the man in the suit, who played the main dollucklan.
Nick Rasna hugs the other two other actors and all of a sudden, crying falls into their hugs: the emotional load of the performance finally fell on him.
“It’s okay,” one woman whistling.
“We know that your heart is good.
“You’ve had reasons for that.”
Later, viewers siege to have a dinner with the actors.
At the same dexict, a white couple from Atlanta is animatedly discussing a spectacle with a black co-couple they just got to know.
Denizya Daplinsky hardly finds words to describe what she feels after the performance.
It’s hard to listen to these terrible, crestinous words, and the actions . ... the people who look like you and who do it because they look like you,” she says.
“After this, I think lynching is still taking place,” she adds, “only it’s just now that it’s what the police are shooting.”
In the other corner of the room, Tyron Brooks took general attention, who at the meal commemorates his work of a human rights activist.
He says that he has already begun to plan the presentation of the next year.
“Strength inspires us, because it not only describes the terrible horror of what we’ve endured but also teaches us,” he says.
“Because if you don’t get out of this lesson, it can happen again.
“Those who don’t study their history, those who don’t know their history are doomed to repetuar.”
The court fined Nabalny for violations on the “agitation subbotnik”
The Simonovsky court of Moscow fined Alexei Navalny for 300 thousand rubles for violating the procedure for organizing a rality.
The protester was found guilty of committing an administrative offense under part 8 of article 20.2 of the administrative code of the Russian Federation “Repeated violation of the procedure for organizing a rally”.
Earlier, the judge of the Simonovsky court of Moscow Khyzyr Mussakayev ended to consider the administrative case against Leonid Volkov - the head of the headquarters of the oppositionist Alexei Navalny, who was prosecuted under the same article as Navalny.
Volukov was found guilty and sentenced to a fine in the amount of 300 thousand rubles.
