New results of tests to estimate the age of asylum-seekers in Sweden
Residing test results for asylum seekers in Sweden, who have been subjected to a new method of medical age assessment, show that more than 80 per cent of those tested have been rated as persons older than 18 years of age, but not everyone trusts the accuracy of these results.
The Swedish National Forensic Agency (Rätstsmedicinalverket) began conducting tests earlier this year.
They have been designed to decelerate an age assessment more accurately, after the Swede Migration Agency (Migrationsverket) was criticized for not being able to determine the real age of some refugees who claimed to be minors.
To date, Migrationsverket has sent 6,880 cases for testing, and the forensic medicine agency has now published 2 481 tests between mid-March and July 31, 2017.
An eighty-perctuary (2002) was evaluated as people of 18 years or older, and in another 25 cases the forensic agency evaluated that the tested person is “probably 18 years or older”.
In the case of 432 cases, the conclusion stated that the person “is probably less than 18”.
Tests are carried out only in cases where the age of a person raises doubts.
According to Johan Göransson of the forensic medicine agency, the results are in line with previous months.
“We don’t see a big difference with the statistics for the past months, the numbers are quite tailored,” he told The Local.
A high proportion of cases in which people were evaluated as who reached the age of 18 does not necessitably indicate that asylum seekers lied about their age.
In May, the agency published the results of 581 tests and reported that in 442 cases “always, the age of being tested for 18 or more”.
However, after reviewing 581 published results, Svenska Dagbladet found that in 243 cases people openly stated that he turned 18 this year.
The method of carrying out a medical age assessment, consisting to make an x-ray of wisdom teeth and ankroibular animate and give them for analysis to dentists and radiologists, also caused criticism in certain instances.
An endocrinologist at Klasin’s Karolina Institute Clad Marcus, part of whose work is an assessment of people’s biological maturity in order to determine whether they went through puberty earlier or later than usual.
“I had a reason to think about it, and I don’t think we need to take advantage of the measurement methods that are being used right now.
Biological age in this way is defined very inaccurately.
If we cann’t define it with certainty, we don’t have to pretend that we can,” he told the Metro newspaper.
Guerssen from the forensic medicine agency spoke in favor of the accuracy of the tests: In the general case, we can be said that the risk is erroneculous to take a child for an adult is small.
With the greatest risk, we are faced, when a person will soon be 18, in which case he is about 10 percent.
I'll think I'm going to run for president
During a working trip to Buryatia, Vladimir Putin said that he is ready to think whether to run for the presidency of Russia in the elections in 2018, reports “Interfax”. “Interfax”.
