Coe has praised the “sincere” an apology of Russia in connection with the doping scandal
Athletics expert Cebestian Coe on Thursday praised what he called a sincere apology from Russia over a doppelgancing scandal, but repeated that now is the inappropriate moment to allow the country to participate in sports competitions again.
The Russian representative of the Athletics Federation Dmitry Shlyakhtin said at the Congress of the International Athletic Federation, which took place on the eve of the athletics world championship, that the removal of his country from sports competitions was fair and that he is determined to deal with doping.
He apologized “to all the athletes from whom gold and silver medals were stitched at the competition.”
Despite its commentaries, the International Association of Athletics Federations Congress voted to maintain the suspension imposed in November 2015, after the World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA), published a State-funded Doping Evidentiary Evidence.
“It seems to me that today's answer, today's report was very sincere," the president of the International Athlelectronic Federation Association of Cow told reporters.
“The whole council and the entire Congress liked it ... that the Russian Federation itself recognizes that it has experienced an extremely heated time and is now doing everything possible to take care of reorganizing the federation.”
Coe said that he also “felt”, that Russia agreed with the criteria for its recovery.
“I believe that this was a very conscruciating day and that we are moving forward, but the Congress supports the recommendations of the special commission, according to which it’s not the time to restore Russia’s rights,” Coe said.
“Over the next few months, we must do everything to resolve this situation.
Our leading principle was our desire to separate clean athletes from the stained system,” he added.
Nineteen Russian athletes, which meet the strict criteria of the International Athletic Federation Association, will compete under a neutral flag in the World Athletics Championship, which will begin on Friday in London.
The Chairman of the special Commission of the International Athletics Federation Association Rune Andersen said on Monday that to restore Russia has yet to fulfill a number of demands.
As he told reporters in London after a report made at the board of the International Athletics Federation Association, narcological testing is still not carried out in sufficient volume, and detached trainers are still doing their job quietly.
