The oil and gas revenues of July exceeded the forecast by 7.4 billion ruble
In fact, the received oil and gas revenues at the end of July of 2017 exceeded the estimate by 7.4 billion rubles, the Ministry of Finance of the Russian Federation reports.
Exceed, according to the agency, occurred mainly due to the higher exchange rate of the U.S. dollar to the ruble, compared with the average monthly value of June, which was used for the estimate, as well as due to the excess of the actual volume of gas production and the volume of exports of petroleum products of the projected levels.
“Deviation of oil and gas revenues of the federal budget from a monthly assessment corresponding to the Federal law on the federal budget for 2017-2019 in August 2017, is projected at +40.2 billion rubles”, – stated in the message of the Ministry of Finance.
Thus, the total volume of funds allocated for the purchase of foreign currency on the domestic foreign exchange market in the period from August 7 to September 6, 2017, is 47.6 billion rubles.
Respectively, the daily volume of purchase of foreign currency will amount to 2.07 billion rub.
The Bank of Russia will be involved in the purchase of foreign-file foreign currency operations by the Ministry of Finance of the Russian Federation.
Each working day during the specified period of the Central Bank will enter into transactions of buying foreign currency at an organized trading of the Moscow Stock Exchange evenly during the trading day.
Purchased foreign currency will be enrolled in the accounts of the Federal Treasury in the Central Bank.
The murder of the journalist emphasizes that it is becoming increasingly dangerous in Mexico.
Employees of the weekly Riodose newspaper are usually found on Wednesdays to discuss plans to cover the latest violent crimes in the state of Sinaloa associated with an organized crime, corrupt officials, or an incessattentive war of drug traffickers.
But today, overwhelmed by their own tragedy, they have come to talk about safety.
They've been told that they have to change their habits.
Two senior reporters have debated what seems to be safer to take the children with them to an office in which a grenade had already been thrown at home in 2009, or leaving them at home.
Security experts wrote on a plaque hanging in the room, three words: the enemies, neutral people, the Allies.
They asked the journalists to name names for each column - there is no need for evidence, there will suffice suspicions and internal sensations.
These are drug traffickers, politicians, businessmen, journalists who are suspected of being kept by the government or cartel, a whole list of scoundrels, because of which working to cover the chaos in Mexico is becoming dangerous.
The violence does not give a respite of resembling, and there are more and more corporal corporates in the country: according to the Investigating Committee of Journalists, at least 25 have since President Enrío Pina Nieto took office in December 2012; 5,589 people after attacks and threats are under federal protection; this year at least seven people have already died in seven.
Among the latter - their publisher and inspirator, co-founder of "Roydoset" Javier Valdez Cardenas.
“The biggest mistake is to live in Mexico to be a journalist,” Waldés wrote in one of his numerous books about violent drug-related crimes.
Its absence is very acute, although his presence everywhere - a large photo of Valdez, showing the middle finger, with the inscription "Justice", hangs on the fabulary of the "Riodose" building; two reporters for 30, Aaron Ibarra and Miriam Ramirez, carry T-shirts that depict his smile, a face in glasses or his signature Panama.
The seminar passes less than two months after his death; reporters discuss their general trauma, their nightmares, insomnia, paranoia.
Although in 2010 an office of the special federal prosecutor was organized to investigate journalistic cases, so far, according to the Committee for the Protection of Journalists, only two cases have been investigated.
As in the case of the majority of thousands of murders involving narocracis and organized crime every year, the murderers of journalists seldom bring to justice.
Journalists of "Riodose" insist that they will write about violence in Sinaloa, although they are killed by grief, although it is now much more dangerous around.
Without information about the killers, without justice, there is very little use at the meetings,” says Ibarra.
“It’s very silly to wasting your time on this seminar,” he said.
"Until we know the reason, we suspect everyone."
On the morning of May 15, Waldez resigned from Riodose’s office in Kuliakane’s state.
He managed to pass only a couple of quarters, before his red Toyota Corolla stopped by two men; he was pulled out of the car and was shot at him 12 times, probably because of the name of the newspaper - which means “Twelfth River”.
The shooter drove his car and smashed it nearby.
Waldez was 50 years old.
He still has a wife and two grown-educated children.
Rumors usually quickly diverge around Kuliakan.
Valdèse is almost all silent.
Since Guzman was arrested and extradited to the U.S. in January last year, Sinaloa has become one of the bloodiest battlefields in the country, as rival factions are struggling to fill a vacuum.
Let's not talk about the fact that every day one or more people are killed on the streets of Sinaloa, or that the cemetery is filled with decorated two-storey mausoleum mausoleums of nucleus, which are greater than the houses of many living people.
Let's forget for a moment that a state of "calm" - it's just when one of the cartels takes control of the seaside state with important ports and ways of drug trafficking leading in the United States.
Despite the fact that Waldez was perfectly aware of the dangers of his work, Ismail Bohorkes, the 60-year-old co-founder and director of Riodose, was crushed with guilt because he could not protect a friend.
He thinks the murder was helped by two mistakes.
At first, an interview with Damaso López was published in February, the leader of one of the rival factions of the cartel, who is at war with Guzman's sons.
The publication could annoyed the sons; the people believed to be members of the gang, bought all copies of the publication, as soon as they were taken to kiosks.
The second mistake was that he didn’t force Valdés to leave the country for the sake of his own security after another newspaper was captured that wrote about the same story.
Valdés was a legend in Mexico and abroad, and his murder was regarded as another milestone in the development of violence against journalists in Mexico.
He left the danger for so a long time, his friends and colleagues thought that he would always.
He was an epitual reporter at Noroeste and in 2003, together with his five-colleague, he founded Riodose, selling his $-Dollar shares.
In Sinaloa "it was impossible to deal with journalism without touching on the topic of drugs," Bohorkes said.
Over time, the newspaper has earned a reputation as a bold and honest publication, sales and advertising revenues grew.
Investigators loved that they could publish courageous reports without afraid of censorship, and the readers were attracted, that in this publication they could read about what no one else dared to write about.
eight years after its founding, Riododus received the prestigious Maria Maurz Cabo Award for reporting in Latin America.
In the same year, Waldez won the International Press Freedom Award for Courage to Investigate the Mortgage Triggering of Mexico, no matter where it leads.
He frankly confessed that he was freaked out.
“I want to live on,” he said at CPJ award.
Narctulating in Sinaloa - "this is a way of life", he said last October in an interview with Rompiciento TV.
"You have to take the tasks that fall on you as a journalist - either so, or if you just wore a fool."
I don’t want to be asked: “What have you done when there were so many deaths... why haven’t you said what was going on?”
Employees of Riodose miss Valdec, a jester who quarreled, like a mover in a port, a friend, a generous hug-and-feeds, a tutor who knew how to avail.
They relied on their order.
He had to always wear his hat.
He had to go to El Guayabo, a bar in front of an office, and always sit there at the same table.
Now they are asked: he was ruined by the love for the newly established order?
His death forced them to doubt their calculations on how it is to do their job and stay alive.
“They don’t like when you’re celebrating their wives, the children, their legal business and clandestine aerobatics.
“These things were at the end of it,” Bohorkes said.
The residue, even in the best of times, was a high level of self-censorship and self-preservation aspirations.
You have to believe our instincts.
Hold yourself away from the fact that it smells bad.
The problem, Riodose' editor Andrés Villareal says, is that "smemory can be deceived ... and what's going on with Javier happens."
The old rules, he and others say, no longer act in Sinaloa - as in Tamaulipas, Veracruz, Guerrero and other states where there is a poisonous mixture of lucrative smuggled queues, weak state institutions and corrupt officials.
Lendshaft is constantly constantly changing.
In the room, where Riodoza employees gathered for a security training, it was suddenly forbidden to pay cell phones: a few days earlier it became known that espionage programs, which are sold exclusively to governments, are used in Mexico to spy on journalists and social activists.
From the outside, the two police officers hide in the shade of the tree from the 104-degree hertiary (40 degrees Celsius).
They were tasked by the state government to guard Riodos’ office, which is located in a four-story building in Kuliakana’s bourgeois neighbourhood.
Some reporters half-jokingly asked whether these cops do not include those 50 per cent of servicemen, about whom the governor himself said that they could not be trusted.
A few months a few months ago, the journalists went into a mountainous area, which is de facto under the control of gangs of narcurgists.
In the next issue "Riodose" counted on three main themes.
This is the murder in Sinaloa brother of former boxing champion Julio César Chávez.
They also had an expiry of government spending, concentrated in the governor's hometown.
And there was a gang kidnapping of people in one of Kuliakan's most expensive restaurants, a block from the prosecutor's office.
It hasn’t officially been announced who was kidnapped or how it happened, so it’d be careful when it’s time to write that everyone in the city knew that it was a favorite restaurant at the same time as drug dealers and authorities.
One of their journals learned from open-source databases that the restaurant had been registered as a politician belonging to the ruling Enstigative-Revolutionary Party (PRI), which dominated at all levels of government for almost the past century.
PRI has recently made high-profficiency accusations of plut from PRI.
Villareal asked journalists to write about previous accidents in this restaurant, including when Guzman’s son “El Chapo” escalated from the wave of the military.
Even a few months ago, the readers were looking for Valdez’s column with the most trustworthy abduction.
“Before, we’d have already known what’s going to happen,” Villarearal, said, nicknamed “El Flaco.”
“Today, all channels of communication with our sources are broken.”
Waldez’s cabinet is now used as a warehouse for posters and stickers with protests against the killings of journalists, because the employees have become what they have never considered to be: activists acting on behalf of the press.
Journalist Miriam Ramirez took several posters and went the next morning for a demonstration from the local prosecutor's office, dedicated to another journalist.
Al Salvador Adame has disappeared in Michoacán in the western country three days after Waldés was killed.
They found a burned-and-fired body, and the authorities said that it was it, on the basis of DNA tests.
Throughout the country, journalists began to speak louder, to organize protests and write “SOS Press” on the pavements and the walls of the houses.
On this day, director Bohorkes was in Washington trying to enclerge with international support for justice in Valdez.
At a meeting with Governor of Sinaloah the day after Valdés, Ramirez accused authorities of spying on reporters and punishing their murders for telling the truth.
After this, she asked not to give her more tasks to illuminate the actions of the government, fearing that the anger would indoctrievance.
Voldez has said many times that journalists in Mexico are “surrounded” with organized crime, its accomplices in the government and indifferatory society.
In his latest book, Narcotic Journalism, he wrote that reporters are being murdered not just by gangs of narcurgists; they do so on the orders of politicians and the security services in collusion with an organized crime.
The Committee for the protection of the press “Articulo 19” believes that half of the attacks on journalists last year were carried out on the orders of the police and government officials.
“In Mexico, you’re dying because they want to shut you up,” Ramirez said.
Of course, the killing frightens people.
Everyone remembers the death of blogger Maria Elizabeth Macyas six years ago in the northern border state of Tamaulipas.
On her body found a merchandise note supposedly written on behalf of the Zetas cartel: “I’m here because of my articles.”
the computer keyboard and headphones lay next to her severed head.
Some publications chose to clapple how the El Norte newspaper in the northern cross-border state of Chihuahua after her correspondent Miroslava Brich was murdered in March after her correspondent Miroslava Bricid was killed.
Others continue to harbour like El Magnán from Nuevo Laredo after the assassination of its leader in 2004.
In 2010, “Diario de Ciudad Juarez” publicly addressed drug cartels using an editor-in-to-date on the first page entitled “What Do You Worth of Us?”
Some reporters have left their native states or in the country at all.
It's an excruciating decision.
In exaltation it is difficult to find a job, and they still specifies the danger on the streets.
And they’re sometimes being murdered, evidently happened to photographer Ruben Espinosa, who was murdered in 2015, along with four women in an apartment in Mexica three months after fleeing Veracruz.
Those who remain and continue to work calculate between risky decisions every day.
Ibarra - who once dreamed of being a poet - admits that he is afraid to write about the drug dealer.
“Mexico goes to hell, and that’s why I became a journalist,” he says.
Last Friday, at midnight, when the last graduation was already ready for printing, Riodose publishers were sitting on the treadmill next to the office and drank beer, when suddenly their phones at the same jerk at the same time.
A series of firefights between opposing gangs and security forces near the resort town of Masatlan left, according to confirmed, 19 corpses.
The war continued to erupt, as promised in a series of messages from cartels found in the area.
Sitting on the sidewalk, they posted the news on the Riodose website via a mobile phone.
The next day you had to rewrite the first page.
There were sirens - another firefight in the vicinity.
Bokhorkes looked ready to defend the cops guarding them.
If they were freaked, they didn't show it.
Sitting under a huge portrait of the deceased founder of their newspaper, whose middle finger was put on display throughout Sinaloa, Employees of Riodos continued his work.
"How do you think about closing," said Bohorkes, "if the very day Javière was murdered, the indivitant asked me to send her on the street?"
Dynosaurus used camouflage to avoid enemies
At first glance, it seems like a statue, albeit terrifyingly terrifying.
The researchers said that the first found remains of an impending rorogar-sized creature discovered by a Canadian minator survived so well that experts called it “Mona Lisa among the Dinosaur.”
It was preserved so well that they were even able to determine that the color of its scaly skin was reddish-brown - and to say that it had, in a way, "dark past".
In a report published in Modern Biology, it was described as “the most well-preserved from Tyreauphore in the history of finds and one of the best specimuli of a Dinosaur in the world.”
