Russian Deputy Prime Minister Mutko: “We will fulfill the requirements of the WADA roadmap in the near future
Russian Deputy Prime Minister Vitaly Mutko said that the World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA), set for the resumption of the Russian Anti-Doping Agency (RUSADA), will be fulfilled in the near future.
“A certain stage in the fulfillment of the requirements has been passed, RUSADA received the right to plan and carry out tests in conjunction with the British Anti-Doping Agency (the UK anti-doping agency).
WADA roadmap has accordingly been renovated," Mutko said on Thursday.
The Prime Minister commented on the second part of WADA’s roadmap to restore the status of compliance with the Code, which enclose the conditions for the resumption of RUSSADA and has recently been published on this organization’s website.
According to Mutko, it does not contain anything that could present difficulties for Russia.
“Everything has been announced, everything will be completed.
And the results will be presented to the International Olympic Committee (IEQ) and WADA in the near future,” he added.
However, one of the requirements included in the list implies that Russian anddo-diping structures should publicly recognize the results of the WADA-ordered investigation by Canadian lawyer Richard McLaren, which claimed that doping in Russian sports is controlled by the state.
Regarding this issue, Mutko said: “We are conducting an investigation because our anti-do-do-doping system has failed.
All measures have been taken.
But there were no state-of-state programs, and we won’t recognize what didn’t happen.”
Mutko repiled the words of Vitaly Smirnov, the head of the Russian independent public anti-doping commission.
On the morning of the same day in an interview with the Russian edition of Rsport, Smyrnov recognized the problems in Russian anti-do-doping structures who had previously taken place, but he added: "We have repeatedly said that the report contains certain controversial positions and provisions.
Unconditionally, no one is going to accept this report,” Smirnov added.
WADA removed RUSADA from doping control in November 2015 after the doping scandal.
However, in June of this year, he was allowed to plan and co-ordinate testing under the supervision of international experts and the British Anti-Doping Agency (UKAD).
After attending the organization's Moscow office in July, WADA CEO Olivier Niggli said that RUSADA is "moving in the right direction" to restore his work.
