Like Glint, Convercent allows companies to send individually-generated employee opinion polls, collect confidential answers in real-truth, and look through thermal maps of problem areas.
It’s also a hotshoe hotline for anonymous text messages, which allows employees to report poor behavior.
And if the top-manager is touched upon, the complaints go directly to the directorate.
“Judgment of Public Opinion Seized Power from Inspectors,” said Patrick Quinlan, the founder and president of Convercent.
If it turns it out that the company does not treat employees or behaves unethically, even if the inspectors do not interfere, the company may face expensive consequences in the form of boycotts from customers, the outflow of employees and lawsuits, Quinlan said.
Rebly Tuesday, a chain of restaurants where more than 25000 Employees Operated in Expenditures, has used Convercent for more than a year to make sure that the employees are aware of the rules and procedures, and to offer an easy way to contact the company’s head office.
Earlier, if the employee wanted to report on the problem, he or she had to look for a phone number or meal of the head office, to file an official complaint, and to hope that she would be taken seriously.
Often it was a frightening and awkward experience, said James Vitrano, the head of the legal management of the company “Rub Tuesday”, who said that there was not a normal way to track employee complaints.
Now, when the company is enjoying Convercent, problems that have previously been humbled from managers sitting in offices in other cities or states – such as discrimination, harassment and unfavorable wages – you can quickly detect and deal with them.
“I can come closer to a holistic, all-to-emphasic understanding of employee situations,” Vitranto said, who is directing Ruby Tuesday’s risk management team.
“And we’re defending our shareholders from lawsuits with our employees.”
According to Quingland, the companies began to seriously treat the ethics, values and involvement of Employees in the year of 2002, after the accounting firm Artur Andersen collapsed due to ethical disturbances as a result of “Enron Cause”.
But it wasn’t when “social networks are widespread,” the companies realized they wouldn’t be able to keep their dirty lingerie away from viral spreading on the net.
“BEFORE we start using technology to control ethics, people used hope as a strategy,” he said.
Both “Glint and Convercent” offer their software as a service, charging companies for the use of their products.
This is a business-model and an opportunity that received the approval of venture capitalists, who supported both startups.
Convercent received $110 million in funding from Sapphire Ventures and Tola Capital, bringing the company’s $247 million share.
“Glint” received $ 10 million in November from “Bessemer Venture Partners”, as a result of which the total capital of the company amounted to $ 60 million.
These investments are hardly astonishing if we take into account the interrelated nature of companies, culture and venture capital.
Growing research demonstrates that today’s Employees Expect More From Their Jobs than they used to.
In markets with tough competition, such as Silicon Valley, high wages and interesting projects, it’s just a necessary minimum.
Employees wish to feel that they are accepted and appreciated, and that they dedicate their time to companies with a positive mission.
“When people are happy to work in the company, feel they are being heard, and that the work they’re delivering results, they’re trying to make such a company more successful,” says Nina McQueen, vice vice president of global benefits and employee satisfaction at LinkedIn, where Convercent is used and “Glint”.
Investors receive income when businesses from their portfolio go well; companies are doing well when the employees are biased and involved.
If the analytical tools of the third party promise to increase the dedication and involvement of the employees, then it is unsurprising that they find support.
According to cultural specialists in the workplace, it is important to have data on the involvement of employees.
But the data is useless, until the company's trustees take action.
In fact, if the company asks employees for feedback, it can give the impression that the changes are coming.
And if the changes do not come quickly enough, or do not occur at all, frustration can develop, and the employees will lose interest.
“If you are going to ask for 4000 sentences, you have to be willing to conduct 4000 conversations,” says Russell Rat, president of advising at Kottter International’s management firm, who’s had to see companies making a mistake by relying too much on data collection.
“Because if you don’t go to an end, the employees will guess, ‘Have you really heard me?
You don't care?
And if you don't care, why don't I have to do anyway?"
Errett said, Madison Reed, which now conducts monthly employee surveys, is in a position to take action on the same day when someone reports a problem with Glint.
And, after collecting feedback from employees dissatisfied with the speed of decision-making and the quality of communication, she was able to reorganize several teams within the company and introduce communication training to solve the problems.
“Ruby Tuesday” receives a new knowledge about its employees and hopes that in the long term this knowledge will help it more effectively retain employees in an industry, known for high staff turnover.
“If you don’t try to create a culture of enlargement, you’re going to lose people,” Vitrano said.
If you’re losing people, you’re losing customers.
And if you are losing your customers, you're losing your company.
