National Unqualified, Municipal and Boiler Workers (GMB) Is Concerned at McVitie’s Facilities in Glasgow
The trade union requires guarantees from managers of the biscuit manufacturer McVitie's in connection with its long-term production obligation at the Glasgow plant.
The Scottish branch GMB reports that the company has ignored the issue of future investments on a velocar site.
The factory, which, among other groceries, Hobnobs and Rich Tea, employs more than 500 people.
Pladis, McVitie’s owner, reports that over the past few years has invested more than £5 million.
The company adds that in the event of any changes in the work of the facility, employees and representatives of the trade union “recognize about them first”.
In a letter to the head of the facility belonging to the company, the Scottish GMB branch demanded from McVite's a response to the concern that the financing would not be allowed to modernize the production lines.
Senior organizer, Drew Duffy, said: “The members of our trade union are deeply concerned that we can’t get the clarity from the general manager of the facility on business needs and the investment plan for the future of Tolkros.”
Families have worked here for many generations, and the concentrating of workers is great in local society and the territory of Greater Glasgow; this is the most important pillar of decent work on the territory of high unemployment and social deprivation.
Thus, in terms of jobs, salaries and proclamation, against the backdrop of Brexite, the growing subsistence minimum and a chronous decline in production, Glasgow’s East Andn desperately desperately desperately desperately needs production at McVitee’s.
According to Pladis, “The leader of the thrash facility only yesterday received a letter from GMB’s Scotch branch and the company will respond immediately, as we appreciate our constant cooperation with employees and representatives of the trade union.”
Two months ago we held a regular meeting in Tolkres with employees and GMB representatives, which openly discussed a number of issues.
We talked with employees that as all companies operating in the most competitive and unstable economic climate, we are constantly revising our actions - and this applies to all our facilities and areas of activity - in order to remain competitive.
“In recent years, we have invested more than £5 million in our vocabulary facility, especially in production lines and improvements in performance.”
We are fully accommodating the value of Tolcross as a production facility, and in the event of any changes in the work at this facility, employees and representatives of the trade union will learn about it first.”
Putin said that the authorities will not give instructions to Rosneft and Gazprom
Volodymyvil Putin said that the issue of gas supplies to the Oriental petro-chemographical company (VNKHC) should be connected by the Kremlin and the Cabinet, but no one will give instructions to Gazprom, like Rosneft.
